  "query_preview": "..\n\nA large upper level trough will move into the Plains today and will \nbe the focus of the weather locally for the next several days. It \nslows it's eastward progress today as additional shortwave energy \nboth from the southern stream and diving southward down the backside \nof the trough helps try to close off the base of the trough tonight. \nThe system will first present us with a few very warm days with near \nrecord highs on Tuesday ahead of the trough. The stacked trough then \ncuts off and slowly drifts over the area through the latter half of \nthe week present a few chances for rain heading into the weekend and \ncooler temps, albeit near normal for early November. \n\nThe upper level ridge axis crossed the area last night opening the \ndoor for southerly flow to kick up and begin the warm advection \nprocess. Combine this with heights building aloft and even with the \nwealth of cloud cover, temps will climb into the 60s today and low \nto mid 70s on Tuesday as the low level jet really ramps up. 850mb \ntemps will rise into the low teens after being around 9C today. \nRecord highs in the mid to upper 70s look to be possible. Winds will \nbe another issue on Tuesday as the low level jet around 35 knots \ndown to 1500ft could be mixed to the surface. Soundings and \nprobabilistic guidance supports gusts to around 30 knots. \n\nWe could see some light rain showers as the warm front lifts through \nthe area tonight. We've seen some blips on radar through the day \nwhich is all virga, but the front will offer better support but \nlocation of the frontal showers this afternoon lines up pretty well \nwith hires models so best chance for any precip would be over the \nSaginaw Valley and Thumb with lesser chances to the SE. Slight \nchance to Chance POPs will continue to cover this portion of the \nevent. Chances go down on Tuesday as we get solidly in the warm \nsector while the warm front lights up and expands across the U.P. \nand northern Great Lakes. \n\nClosed and occluding upper low then lifts into the upper MS Valley \nTuesday night into Wednesday which will push the moisture axis \nthrough lower MI bringing widespread rain to the area through the \nday. Cold front pushes through early Wednesday night bringing \nanother line of showers through the area before we dry out some the \nrest of the night. Dewpoints will rise to around 60F and PWATs climb \nto around 1.3 inches so there will be some moisture to work with. \nQPF looks lean though without greater forcing as the instability \ngets held southeast of us and the low tracks to the north. Looking \nfor generally around a quarter inch. \n\nDry slotting in between short wave troughs rotating around the \nbroader low, will lead to a drier day Thursday. Thermal trough will \nbe over the area with 850mb temps down to 4-6C which will knock \nhighs back to around 60F during the day, which is still above \nnormal. The longwave trough itself then drifts over the region on \nFriday with residual moisture axis bringing another round of light \nrain or drizzle through the day.",
